WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.589
Imagine two treatments, A and B, that were never tested in the same trial.

00:00:05.589 --> 00:00:09.456
For treatment A, you have the data for every patient.

00:00:09.456 --> 00:00:15.348
For treatment B, you only have what its trial published: counts and averages.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:18.762
No shared control group links the two trials.

00:00:18.762 --> 00:00:22.646
This situation is called an unanchored comparison.

00:00:22.646 --> 00:00:26.398
This lesson teaches one way to make that comparison.

00:00:26.398 --> 00:00:31.867
It is called multilevel unanchored meta-regression, or M L U M R.

00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:38.833
We will build the ideas one small step at a time, and then use the M L U M R package for R.

00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:46.053
The lesson follows the development version of the package, which is heading toward version zero point two.

00:00:46.053 --> 00:00:51.644
The blue trial, trial A, recorded each patient's outcome and characteristics.

00:00:51.644 --> 00:00:56.466
The orange trial, trial B, reported only totals and summaries.

00:00:56.466 --> 00:01:00.902
Our outcome is a harmful event, so a lower risk is better.

00:01:00.902 --> 00:01:04.169
Every number you see is made up for teaching.

00:01:04.169 --> 00:01:07.154
None of it is a real clinical result.

00:01:07.154 --> 00:01:11.080
Some patients carry a marker that raises their risk.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:16.221
The two trials enrolled very different shares of patients with the marker.

00:01:16.221 --> 00:01:18.791
Look first at the crude difference.

00:01:18.791 --> 00:01:22.332
It compares each treatment inside its own trial.

00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:24.935
Now make the two trials more alike.

00:01:24.935 --> 00:01:28.971
The crude difference changed, yet neither treatment changed.

00:01:28.971 --> 00:01:31.097
Only the patients changed.

00:01:31.097 --> 00:01:39.605
The target difference did not move, because it compares both treatments in one chosen population, called the target population.

00:01:39.605 --> 00:01:44.679
Keeping those two questions apart is where population adjustment begins.

00:01:44.679 --> 00:01:48.400
Move the two trial sliders, then the target slider.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.895
Which difference reacts to each one?

00:01:50.895 --> 00:01:56.836
Removing the need for a shared control group does not remove the need for assumptions.

00:01:56.836 --> 00:02:08.228
For an unanchored comparison to work, patients with the same characteristics must be expected to have the same outcome on a given treatment, whichever trial enrolled them.

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:17.256
That means measuring, before treatment, every factor that affects the outcome, including factors that change how well a treatment works.

00:02:17.256 --> 00:02:25.759
The patients in the two trials must also overlap, and the treatments, outcomes and follow-up must mean the same thing in both.

00:02:25.759 --> 00:02:31.937
Suppose trial B also differed in something nobody measured, such as the quality of care.

00:02:31.937 --> 00:02:35.515
We can add that as a hidden shift in trial B's risk.

00:02:35.515 --> 00:02:37.995
Trial B's reported risk goes up.

00:02:37.995 --> 00:02:48.004
But trial B has only one starting level for its risk, so it cannot tell us how much of that rise came from the treatment and how much came from the trial.

00:02:48.004 --> 00:02:53.313
Adjusting for the marker does not help, because the marker was not the problem.

00:02:53.313 --> 00:03:02.821
The same thing happens with a missing risk factor, a different way of measuring the outcome, or a model that predicts badly outside the data.

00:03:02.821 --> 00:03:07.027
More patients can make a biased answer look more precise.

00:03:07.027 --> 00:03:10.603
More careful computation cannot fix it either.

00:03:10.603 --> 00:03:16.350
Reading the result as a causal effect needs assumptions that the data cannot check.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.253
Slide the hidden difference through zero.

00:03:19.253 --> 00:03:23.916
Can the reported risk alone tell you how much came from the treatment?

00:03:23.916 --> 00:03:29.663
The outcome model gives each treatment its own starting level, called an intercept.

00:03:29.663 --> 00:03:38.648
Slopes, also called coefficients, describe how much a patient characteristic, called a covariate, raises or lowers the risk.

00:03:38.648 --> 00:03:44.967
A covariate that affects the outcome, like our marker, is called a prognostic factor.

00:03:44.967 --> 00:03:56.803
The shared prognostic factor assumption, or S P F A, says that both treatments have the same slopes on the scale where the model is a straight-line, called the link scale.

00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:59.269
Here that scale is the log odds.

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:05.761
Each line on the chart shows one treatment's log odds, for patients without the marker and with it.

00:04:05.761 --> 00:04:10.030
Both slopes are two point four, so the lines are parallel.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:14.075
The gap between them is the same with and without the marker.

00:04:14.075 --> 00:04:17.249
That gap is the conditional log odds ratio.

00:04:17.249 --> 00:04:22.834
It compares the model's predictions for patients who share the same marker status.

00:04:22.834 --> 00:04:26.686
It does not mean anyone was seen under both treatments.

00:04:26.686 --> 00:04:29.540
Here, it does not depend on the marker.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.253
Now give trial B a different slope.

00:04:32.253 --> 00:04:34.759
The lines are no longer parallel.

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:41.984
The gap now depends on the marker, so the marker changes how well treatment A works compared with treatment B.

00:04:41.984 --> 00:04:45.436
Statisticians call this effect modification.

00:04:45.436 --> 00:04:50.182
This is the relaxed model, where each treatment has its own slopes.

00:04:50.182 --> 00:04:52.900
That flexibility comes at a price.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:56.538
Trial A's patient data can estimate trial A's slopes.

00:04:56.538 --> 00:05:09.198
Trial B's slopes can only come from its published summaries, mainly the differences between its subgroups, and from the prior, which is what you assume about a value before seeing the data.

00:05:09.198 --> 00:05:14.674
Writing down a relaxed model does not mean the data can actually estimate it.

00:05:14.674 --> 00:05:18.649
Set trial B's slope back to two point four, then lower it.

00:05:18.649 --> 00:05:22.558
Which odds ratio stays the same, and which one changes?

00:05:22.558 --> 00:05:28.266
Trial B only reports averages, like the share of its patients who had the event.

00:05:28.266 --> 00:05:35.452
To connect those averages to a model for individual patients, the model has to average in the same way.

00:05:35.452 --> 00:05:42.655
It predicts the risk for each kind of patient, and then averages those risks over trial B's population.

00:05:42.655 --> 00:05:45.819
This averaging step is called integration.

00:05:45.819 --> 00:05:50.387
At first, every patient has the same covariate value, zero.

00:05:50.387 --> 00:05:55.650
Then the risk of the average patient and the average risk are the same thing.

00:05:55.650 --> 00:06:00.974
Now spread the patients out, while keeping their average exactly at zero.

00:06:00.974 --> 00:06:02.607
The curve bends.

00:06:02.607 --> 00:06:07.464
Patients above the average gain more risk than patients below it lose.

00:06:07.464 --> 00:06:13.424
So the average risk rises, while the risk of the average patient stays where it was.

00:06:13.424 --> 00:06:18.967
Replacing a whole population with one average patient gives a different answer.

00:06:18.967 --> 00:06:22.404
The dots are the points used to do the averaging.

00:06:22.404 --> 00:06:26.522
Here they are simply spaced evenly, so you can see them.

00:06:26.522 --> 00:06:34.607
The package instead uses Sobol points, an evenly spread sequence drawn from the covariate distributions you choose.

00:06:34.607 --> 00:06:37.428
These points are a calculation tool.

00:06:37.428 --> 00:06:39.597
They are not real patients.

00:06:39.597 --> 00:06:43.418
With more points, the average becomes more accurate.

00:06:43.418 --> 00:06:51.179
In a real analysis, check the integration, and check that the effect you report stops changing when you add points.

00:06:51.179 --> 00:07:01.833
The package's integration check compares covariate means and standard deviations on two grids, and stable values there do not prove that the effect is stable.

00:07:01.833 --> 00:07:07.347
Below the chart, you can run this same calculation in R, right in your browser.

00:07:07.347 --> 00:07:12.860
Spread the patients out, then compare four points with one hundred twenty-eight.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:19.455
Which change moves the average risk itself, and which only makes the calculation more accurate?

00:07:19.455 --> 00:07:26.040
A table of covariate means and standard deviations does not fully describe a population.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.198
Picture two markers, each present in half of the patients.

00:07:30.198 --> 00:07:39.713
If the markers are unrelated, the four kinds of patient are equally common: neither marker, only the first, only the second, or both.

00:07:39.713 --> 00:07:43.140
Now make the two markers tend to appear together.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:46.631
More patients now have both markers, or neither.

00:07:46.631 --> 00:07:51.160
Each marker is still present in exactly half of the patients.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:08:01.025
Yet the average risk changes, because the risk curve bends: having both markers adds more risk than the two markers add one at a time.

00:08:01.025 --> 00:08:06.869
With a negative correlation, patients tend to have exactly one of the two markers.

00:08:06.869 --> 00:08:11.408
The same published summaries can hide different average risks.

00:08:11.408 --> 00:08:19.543
By default, the package estimates how the covariates go together from trial A's patient data, and uses that for trial B.

00:08:19.543 --> 00:08:26.016
Carrying it over is an assumption, so look at the result and try other plausible values.

00:08:26.016 --> 00:08:32.516
One more detail: when the shares are not both one half, some correlations are impossible.

00:08:32.516 --> 00:08:37.341
Here every value works, because both shares are exactly one half.

00:08:37.341 --> 00:08:42.205
Keep each marker at half the patients and change only the correlation.

00:08:42.205 --> 00:08:44.572
Why does the average risk move?

00:08:44.572 --> 00:08:49.219
An estimand is a precise statement of what you want to estimate.

00:08:49.219 --> 00:08:54.726
It names the two treatments, the outcome, the population and the effect scale.

00:08:54.726 --> 00:08:57.571
For survival, it also names a time.

00:08:57.571 --> 00:09:04.118
The population could be trial A's patients, trial B's patients, or another group you care about.

00:09:04.118 --> 00:09:09.298
Choose it from the decision you need to make, not from a software default.

00:09:09.298 --> 00:09:12.132
Both treatments now share their slopes.

00:09:12.132 --> 00:09:17.421
Move the target population from mostly without the marker to mostly with it.

00:09:17.421 --> 00:09:24.599
The conditional odds ratio is the same with and without the marker, but the population odds ratio moves.

00:09:24.599 --> 00:09:28.809
This surprising behavior is called non-collapsibility.

00:09:28.809 --> 00:09:34.142
It happens even without effect modification, and even without confounding.

00:09:34.142 --> 00:09:41.361
The risk difference also changes, because it depends on the baseline risks and on the mix of patients.

00:09:41.361 --> 00:09:49.999
The package fits a Bayesian model, which produces thousands of plausible sets of parameter values, called posterior draws.

00:09:49.999 --> 00:09:56.094
Inside every draw it predicts, averages and compares, and only then summarizes.

00:09:56.094 --> 00:10:06.764
For trial B's own population, subgroups are weighted by their number of patients for binary and continuous outcomes, and by exposure time for counts.

00:10:06.764 --> 00:10:12.412
These population weights are not the same thing as how precise each subgroup is.

00:10:12.412 --> 00:10:16.570
If you supply your own target rows, each row counts equally.

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:23.053
And to report an odds ratio, exponentiate each log odds ratio draw first, then summarize.

00:10:23.053 --> 00:10:27.953
Exponentiating the average log odds ratio gives a different number.

00:10:27.953 --> 00:10:35.187
You can ask for effects in trial A's population, in trial B's population, or in your own target population.

00:10:35.187 --> 00:10:38.320
A target changes the question, not the data.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:42.300
It adds no outcomes, and it does not refit the model.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:48.934
Effects for one particular kind of patient are a separate request, called conditional effects.

00:10:48.934 --> 00:10:52.506
Compare target shares of zero, one half and one.

00:10:52.506 --> 00:10:57.987
Why do the conditional and population odds ratios agree at zero and at one?

00:10:57.987 --> 00:11:04.665
Here is a question about the relaxed model: can trial B's summaries pin down trial B's own slope?

00:11:04.665 --> 00:11:12.667
To see this clearly, we switch to a continuous outcome with a straight-line model, which is called an identity link.

00:11:12.667 --> 00:11:20.298
Each subgroup row, one line of trial B's published table, reports its mean outcome and how precise that mean is.

00:11:20.298 --> 00:11:26.628
A row whose patients average zero on the covariate tells us the height of the line at zero.

00:11:26.628 --> 00:11:29.955
It cannot tell us both the height and the slope.

00:11:29.955 --> 00:11:34.172
Add a second, separate row at the same covariate value.

00:11:34.172 --> 00:11:38.925
There are now two rows, but they still point in only one direction.

00:11:38.925 --> 00:11:42.122
They make the estimate at zero more precise.

00:11:42.122 --> 00:11:44.328
They do not reveal the slope.

00:11:44.328 --> 00:11:49.645
These are two different groups of patients, not the same row counted twice.

00:11:49.645 --> 00:11:53.176
Now give the two rows different covariate values.

00:11:53.176 --> 00:11:57.126
Now the data can separate the intercept from the slope.

00:11:57.126 --> 00:11:59.637
Move the two rows close together.

00:11:59.637 --> 00:12:06.028
Technically, the slope is still identified, meaning the data can pin it down in principle.

00:12:06.028 --> 00:12:10.653
But predicting a target far from both rows becomes very uncertain.

00:12:10.653 --> 00:12:14.683
Being identified and being precise are different things.

00:12:14.683 --> 00:12:18.604
For a straight-line model, this reasoning is exact.

00:12:18.604 --> 00:12:26.935
With a curved link, as for binary or count outcomes, or a log link, the spread of patients inside each row matters too.

00:12:26.935 --> 00:12:35.923
So when there are enough rows, the package's subgroup check only describes them, and reports its flag as missing rather than as a pass.

00:12:35.923 --> 00:12:39.103
It does not accept survival data at all.

00:12:39.103 --> 00:12:44.157
Compare one row, two rows in the same place, and two separated rows.

00:12:44.157 --> 00:12:46.384
Then move the target to zero.

00:12:46.384 --> 00:12:50.828
Now let's turn these ideas into a real analysis with the package.

00:12:50.828 --> 00:12:53.108
The chart shows six steps.

00:12:53.108 --> 00:13:00.290
Further down this page, a code cell runs real package code, and the real Stan model, inside your browser.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.985
The first step needs no code at all.

00:13:02.985 --> 00:13:10.441
Write down the two treatments, the outcome, the target population and the effect scale before you touch the data.

00:13:10.441 --> 00:13:12.494
Next, prepare the data.

00:13:12.494 --> 00:13:19.502
One function describes trial A's patient data, and the package calls treatment A the index treatment.

00:13:19.502 --> 00:13:25.572
Another describes trial B's published summaries, and calls treatment B the comparator.

00:13:25.572 --> 00:13:27.967
A third function combines them.

00:13:27.967 --> 00:13:30.507
Column names are written in quotes.

00:13:30.507 --> 00:13:36.125
If trial B reports subgroups, each patient must belong to exactly one of them.

00:13:36.125 --> 00:13:44.142
Tables that overlap, such as one by age and another by sex, cannot be stacked as if they were separate groups.

00:13:44.142 --> 00:13:50.155
Then describe trial B's covariates with distributions, and add integration points.

00:13:50.155 --> 00:13:54.029
Choose a distribution that fits each covariate's range.

00:13:54.029 --> 00:14:04.270
A covariate that is itself a proportion, such as the share of skin affected, should not get a normal distribution that allows values below zero.

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:10.778
Check the integration, and if you plan a relaxed model, check what the subgroups can identify.

00:14:10.778 --> 00:14:16.273
Now fit the model, with the priors written out, a seed, and enough sampling.

00:14:16.273 --> 00:14:21.181
The default engine is R Stan, and command Stan R also works.

00:14:21.181 --> 00:14:28.342
In the relaxed model, trial B's slopes can have their own prior, which makes sensitivity analysis easy.

00:14:28.342 --> 00:14:32.869
Choose prior scales that make sense for each covariate's units.

00:14:32.869 --> 00:14:35.974
Before trusting any number, check the fit.

00:14:35.974 --> 00:14:41.950
The fitting algorithm, called the sampler, runs several independent chains of draws.

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:49.753
The package checks those chains when it finishes and warns about problems, and the summary shows the checks again.

00:14:49.753 --> 00:14:57.751
Centering the covariates, which the package does by default, and the optional Q R setting can both help the sampler.

00:14:57.751 --> 00:15:08.897
With centering, the intercept describes a patient with average covariates, so your intercept prior applies to that patient, not to one whose covariates are all zero.

00:15:08.897 --> 00:15:13.828
With Q R, your priors still apply to the original coefficients.

00:15:13.828 --> 00:15:18.904
Neither setting creates information, or makes the trials comparable.

00:15:18.904 --> 00:15:23.313
Finally, ask for the population and the effect you planned.

00:15:23.313 --> 00:15:27.002
The effect called L O R is a log odds ratio.

00:15:27.002 --> 00:15:30.121
The package also offers two quick benchmarks.

00:15:30.121 --> 00:15:34.886
The naive comparison simply compares the two trials as they are.

00:15:34.886 --> 00:15:45.026
S T C, the simulated treatment comparison, fits a regression to trial A, and predicts what treatment A would do in trial B's population.

00:15:45.026 --> 00:15:49.512
It then compares that prediction with trial B's reported outcome.

00:15:49.512 --> 00:15:57.172
That question does not need equal slopes, but carrying its answer to another population needs more justification.

00:15:57.172 --> 00:15:59.810
Step through the six code panels.

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:04.372
Then run the cell below: first the R code, then the Stan fit.

00:16:04.372 --> 00:16:10.802
The package handles four kinds of outcome: binary, continuous, counts and survival.

00:16:10.802 --> 00:16:21.313
Most function names are shared, but survival uses its own function for trial B's data, and each outcome needs different data and reports different effects.

00:16:21.313 --> 00:16:23.721
A binary outcome is yes or no.

00:16:23.721 --> 00:16:30.705
Trial A gives each patient's result, and trial B gives its number of events and number of patients.

00:16:30.705 --> 00:16:36.702
The logit link is the default, with probit and complementary log-log as alternatives.

00:16:36.702 --> 00:16:44.309
Risk differences, risk ratios and log odds ratios are all built from risks averaged over the population.

00:16:44.309 --> 00:16:47.548
A continuous outcome uses a normal model.

00:16:47.548 --> 00:16:52.813
Trial B must report its mean outcome and the standard error of that mean.

00:16:52.813 --> 00:16:57.974
Do not confuse this with the standard deviation of individual outcomes.

00:16:57.974 --> 00:17:08.572
For a simple unweighted mean of independent patients, divide the standard deviation of individual outcomes by the square root of the number of patients.

00:17:08.572 --> 00:17:14.932
If the paper already gives the standard error, use it as it is, without dividing again.

00:17:14.932 --> 00:17:23.239
An adjusted, weighted, clustered or repeated-measure estimate needs the standard error that belongs to its analysis.

00:17:23.239 --> 00:17:27.684
The mean must also describe what the model predicts for that row.

00:17:27.684 --> 00:17:38.734
An adjusted mean standardized to another population is a different quantity from the row's own mean, and a correct standard error does not repair a mismatched mean.

00:17:38.734 --> 00:17:46.149
The covariate standard deviations are a different thing: they describe how the covariates are spread.

00:17:46.149 --> 00:17:53.404
Even with a log link, give trial B's mean and its standard error on the original scale, not the log scale.

00:17:53.404 --> 00:17:57.103
Counts need exposure, such as years of follow-up.

00:17:57.103 --> 00:18:01.214
The effect is a rate ratio, where one means no difference.

00:18:01.214 --> 00:18:04.790
Keep the exposure units the same in both trials.

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:16.792
And if follow-up time depends on the covariates, trial B's covariate summaries should weight each patient by follow-up time, which ordinary published averages usually do not.

00:18:16.792 --> 00:18:24.260
Survival outcomes use event times, plus censored times for patients whose follow-up ended before an event.

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:32.896
For trial B, you supply event times reconstructed from its published survival curve, together with covariate summaries.

00:18:32.896 --> 00:18:41.125
Reconstruction does not recover trial B's individual covariates, and its own uncertainty is not carried into the fit.

00:18:41.125 --> 00:18:47.722
The package offers several parametric and flexible baseline shapes, each with its own rules.

00:18:47.722 --> 00:18:52.504
Open the list of survival distributions in the panel to see them.

00:18:52.504 --> 00:18:54.348
Pick an outcome type.

00:18:54.348 --> 00:18:59.539
What must trial B report, and which value of the effect means no difference?

00:18:59.539 --> 00:19:08.721
This survival example has two risk groups: patients with the marker, whose hazard is higher, and patients without it.

00:19:08.721 --> 00:19:18.471
Survival in each group follows a simple exponential curve, and within each group, the hazard ratio of A versus B is zero point six five.

00:19:18.471 --> 00:19:26.001
This is a conditional hazard ratio: it compares the treatments for patients in the same risk group.

00:19:26.001 --> 00:19:30.635
The target population starts as an equal mix of the two groups.

00:19:30.635 --> 00:19:33.315
Now move forward through follow-up.

00:19:33.315 --> 00:19:39.157
High-risk patients have their events sooner, so they leave the group still at risk.

00:19:39.157 --> 00:19:44.434
The mix of survivors changes, and it changes faster under treatment B.

00:19:44.434 --> 00:19:50.779
The population hazard is an average over the survivors, not over everyone who started.

00:19:50.779 --> 00:19:59.332
That is why the population hazard ratio changes over time, even though the hazard ratio within each risk group stays constant.

00:19:59.332 --> 00:20:02.864
Now remove the difference between the risk groups.

00:20:02.864 --> 00:20:11.947
With identical risk groups, the survivors never change their mix, and the population hazard ratio stays at zero point six five.

00:20:11.947 --> 00:20:20.903
Restricted mean survival time, or R M S T, is the area under a survival curve up to a chosen time, called the horizon.

00:20:20.903 --> 00:20:26.856
In plain terms, it is the average time patients stay event free up to that horizon.

00:20:26.856 --> 00:20:33.186
The shaded area between the two curves is the R M S T difference, measured in months.

00:20:33.186 --> 00:20:36.490
Change the horizon, and you change the question.

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:47.309
For the same outcome, time origin, treatments and target population, only compare R M S T results that use the same horizon and the same time units.

00:20:47.309 --> 00:20:54.683
In the package, each trial gets its own baseline shape by default, whenever the distribution has a shape.

00:20:54.683 --> 00:21:04.741
Sharing one shape is a stronger assumption, but separate shapes also assume that each shape travels with its treatment, so fit both and compare.

00:21:04.741 --> 00:21:08.377
A population hazard ratio always needs a time.

00:21:08.377 --> 00:21:18.400
Depending on the model, the package gives it at time zero, at a time you choose, rounded to its nearest prediction time, or as a whole curve over time.

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:27.193
For accelerated failure time models, the population number is a time ratio only when the slopes and the shape are both shared.

00:21:27.193 --> 00:21:33.389
Otherwise, the package labels it differently, so it is not mistaken for a time ratio.

00:21:33.389 --> 00:21:44.511
Patients still event free when follow-up ends, events known only to fall within a time window, and patients who join follow-up late each need their own likelihood terms.

00:21:44.511 --> 00:21:47.818
A simple count of events cannot replace them.

00:21:47.818 --> 00:21:53.792
And coding censoring correctly does not show that censoring is unrelated to the outcome.

00:21:53.792 --> 00:21:57.058
Compare zero, twelve and twenty-four months.

00:21:57.058 --> 00:22:00.351
Then remove the risk difference between groups.

00:22:00.351 --> 00:22:04.165
What happens to the population hazard ratio, and why?

00:22:04.165 --> 00:22:06.799
Back to the straight-line example.

00:22:06.799 --> 00:22:12.231
With one row at zero, a target at one depends on a slope the data cannot see.

00:22:12.231 --> 00:22:19.136
The band shows the answer using normal priors centered at zero, on both the intercept and the slope.

00:22:19.136 --> 00:22:21.067
Make the prior tighter.

00:22:21.067 --> 00:22:24.094
The interval at the target shrinks a lot.

00:22:24.094 --> 00:22:26.407
No new patient was observed.

00:22:26.407 --> 00:22:31.308
The answer is narrower only because the assumption became stronger.

00:22:31.308 --> 00:22:34.116
Now move the target to where the row is.

00:22:34.116 --> 00:22:39.622
Here the data alone pin down the target, even though the slope is still unknown.

00:22:39.622 --> 00:22:44.655
Pinned down does not mean certain: the target still has an interval.

00:22:44.655 --> 00:22:48.057
And this argument needs the straight-line model.

00:22:48.057 --> 00:22:52.176
With a curved link, matching the row's averages is not enough.

00:22:52.176 --> 00:22:58.291
So a verdict about the coefficients is not automatically a verdict about your target.

00:22:58.291 --> 00:23:06.913
The package's identification check screens the coefficients, not your target, so ask this question about the target you will report.

00:23:06.913 --> 00:23:11.492
Check prior sensitivity for the target you will actually report.

00:23:11.492 --> 00:23:20.509
Results in trial B's population can look stable, while results in trial A's population, or in a new target, still depend on the prior.

00:23:20.509 --> 00:23:27.366
Try several reasonable priors, see how far the result moves under each, and report what you find.

00:23:27.366 --> 00:23:31.961
Never pick the prior that happens to give the narrowest interval.

00:23:31.961 --> 00:23:35.639
Change the prior with the target at zero, then at one.

00:23:35.639 --> 00:23:38.076
Repeat with two separated rows.

00:23:38.076 --> 00:23:42.477
Which narrowing comes from data, and which from assumptions?

00:23:42.477 --> 00:23:46.164
Suppose a model hands you a neat table of results.

00:23:46.164 --> 00:23:49.482
Before reading it, look at the sampling checks.

00:23:49.482 --> 00:23:51.212
Did every chain run?

00:23:51.212 --> 00:23:57.554
Were there divergent transitions, where the sampler could not explore part of the posterior?

00:23:57.554 --> 00:24:05.756
Then check R hat, the effective sample size, which says how many independent draws the chains are worth, and tree depth.

00:24:05.756 --> 00:24:09.137
A missing or infinite check is not good news.

00:24:09.137 --> 00:24:15.910
Raising a sampler setting can help, but it does not fix a badly shaped posterior by itself.

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:21.894
R hat compares the chains with each other, and values close to one mean they agree.

00:24:21.894 --> 00:24:25.896
When chains settle in different places, R hat grows.

00:24:25.896 --> 00:24:31.464
A value of infinity usually means the chains got stuck, each at its own value.

00:24:31.464 --> 00:24:36.648
Next, keep what the data can tell you apart from how well the computer ran.

00:24:36.648 --> 00:24:44.208
When the subgroup check reports a missing flag for a binary outcome, it is holding back a verdict on purpose.

00:24:44.208 --> 00:24:52.519
With a curved link, subgroup means alone cannot show what the rows identify, because the spread inside each row matters too.

00:24:52.519 --> 00:24:55.600
Check the numerical integration on its own.

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:58.637
More points reduce approximation error.

00:24:58.637 --> 00:25:05.561
But neither a stable grid nor good sampling shows that the covariate distributions you chose are right.

00:25:05.561 --> 00:25:17.623
Look at how the results depend on trial B's slope priors, on the target population, on covariate overlap, on the outcome model, and on plausible differences between the trials.

00:25:17.623 --> 00:25:24.667
When some posterior draws give no usable value, the package leaves them out of the summary and warns.

00:25:24.667 --> 00:25:27.071
Keep that warning in your report.

00:25:27.071 --> 00:25:35.736
A survival median that falls beyond the time grid is a separate issue, reported as the chance that the median was not reached.

00:25:35.736 --> 00:25:41.890
Never quietly drop an inconvenient check, or present an incomplete summary as complete.

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:50.126
Finally, a better predictive score, such as L O O, only says which model predicts these observations better.

00:25:50.126 --> 00:26:00.211
Compare two models by the paired difference of their pointwise scores and its standard error, not by whether two separate intervals overlap.

00:26:00.211 --> 00:26:05.168
It cannot tell you whether a hidden difference between the trials exists.

00:26:05.168 --> 00:26:10.277
Choose each problem, guess the next step, then reveal the explanation.

00:26:10.277 --> 00:26:18.279
The point of M L U M R is one explicit model that links individual patient data with published summaries.

00:26:18.279 --> 00:26:26.701
It lets you ask what both treatments would do in a population you specify, as long as the model and its assumptions hold.

00:26:26.701 --> 00:26:32.126
The research on this method describes extensions to more than two treatments.

00:26:32.126 --> 00:26:37.473
The package covered here compares one index treatment with one comparator.

00:26:37.473 --> 00:26:42.666
It does not fit networks of many treatments, or random study effects.

00:26:42.666 --> 00:26:51.486
Some function names look like those in the multi N M A package, but the models and the fitted objects are not interchangeable.

00:26:51.486 --> 00:26:58.837
Before you report an analysis, name the treatments, the outcome, the target population and the effect scale.

00:26:58.837 --> 00:27:06.244
Explain where the data came from, how well the covariates overlap, and that the subgroups do not overlap.

00:27:06.244 --> 00:27:13.576
Say which covariate distributions and priors you used, and whether slopes were shared or separate.

00:27:13.576 --> 00:27:19.157
Show the sampling checks, the integration checks and the sensitivity analyses.

00:27:19.157 --> 00:27:23.085
For survival, state every time and every horizon.

00:27:23.085 --> 00:27:30.974
The chart shows what a good report gives: an estimate, its interval, and the population it applies to.

00:27:30.974 --> 00:27:36.311
It comes from the companion script's native fits, not from the browser cell.

00:27:36.311 --> 00:27:49.039
To go further, the package website has an article for each outcome type, and the package ships four example datasets, on psoriasis, multiple myeloma, shoulder surgery and tooth decay.

00:27:49.039 --> 00:27:53.776
Test yourself with the ten questions, and read the capstone below them.

00:27:53.776 --> 00:27:59.997
Then revisit any chapter, and try to predict what will change before you move a control.

00:27:59.997 --> 00:28:01.973
Answer the ten questions.

00:28:01.973 --> 00:28:06.810
Then pick a chapter whose chart you can now explain in your own words.
